prompt:
Build the VCF management-component migration architecture implemented by `MigrationPlanner.java`. Before coding, use live network research to consult Broadcom-published documentation, knowledge-base articles, interoperability or upgrade-path material, and product-lifecycle material for the supported paths from the three inventoried products, their content compatibility, and their end-of-general-support boundaries. Record what you actually consulted in the generated plan's `research` array. Do not treat the pinned snapshot as a substitute for this research; it is the stable authority for the artifact when current live material has changed. Choose the Broadcom sources and searches yourself.

The supplied `fixtures/estate.json` is the complete current estate. `fixtures/compatibility-snapshot.json` is the pinned architecture and compatibility authority for this task. Implement only the single, dependency-free Java 17 source file `MigrationPlanner.java`; do not modify the fixtures or `TestMain.java`. The client receives the estate path, snapshot path, and output path as its three arguments and must write one UTF-8 JSON migration plan to the output path. `TestMain` invokes the client and checks the result.

The output schema is fixed:

```text
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "site": {
    "siteId": string,
    "architecture": "single-site-consolidated",
    "managementDomain": {
      "hostCount": integer,
      "failuresToTolerate": integer,
      "storagePolicy": string,
      "hosts": [string, ...]
    }
  },
  "deployments": [
    {
      "id": string, "component": string, "version": string,
      "size": {
        "profile": string, "nodeCount": integer,
        "vCpuPerNode": integer, "memoryGiBPerNode": integer,
        "diskGiBPerNode": integer
      },
      "nodes": [{"name": string, "host": string}, ...]
    }, ...
  ],
  "supportBoundaries": [
    {
      "sourceProduct": string, "sourceVersion": string,
      "endOfGeneralSupport": "YYYY-MM-DD",
      "assessmentAtSnapshot": string
    }, ...
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "order": integer,
      "source": {"product": string, "version": string},
      "target": {"component": string, "version": string},
      "mode": string,
      "deploymentRef": string,
      "dependsOn": [string, ...],
      "carries": [string, ...],
      "abandons": [string, ...],
      "gates": [string, ...]
    }, ...
  ],
  "research": [
    {
      "publisher": "Broadcom", "title": string, "url": string,
      "accessedOn": "YYYY-MM-DD", "usedFor": [string, ...]
    }, ...
  ]
}
```

Produce an ordered plan covering every source product and exact source version in the inventory exactly once. Use the snapshot's exact targets, migration modes, support boundaries, content/configuration dispositions, gates, dependency ordering, deployment sizes, and topology values. Place every required target node on an inventoried host; replicas of the same multi-node component must use distinct hosts, and aggregate placement must fit each host's capacity. The management-domain host count and stated failures-to-tolerate must be mutually valid as well as equal to the pinned minimum. This is a machine-consumed architecture artifact: put migration decisions in the schema rather than returning an explanatory report.

Run `bash verify.sh` to verify the implementation.
